WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:05.990
Reportedly written in just six days by John
Hughes, Ferris Bueller's Day Off captures

00:00:05.990 --> 00:00:10.139
a single, magical day in the life of a high
schooler who understands, well…

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.200
“Life moves pretty fast.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.210
If you don’t stop and look around once in
awhile, you could miss it.”

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:23.840
Ferris outsmarts his parents and his principal,
steals a classic car, gets the girl, gives

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.900
his best friend self-confidence, dines in
a fancy restaurant, takes in a baseball game,

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.070
and even sings a Beatles song on a parade
float.

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:32.430
“They all adore him.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:36.300
They think he’s a righteous dude.”

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.120
So why is it that we only ever got that single
movie?

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.920
Here are some reasons why we never got to
see a Ferris Bueller sequel.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:44.160
“Bueller?

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:46.720
Bueller?

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:49.120
Bueller?”

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.460
It didn't need one

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:55.590
Director John Hughes and star Matthew Broderick
stayed in touch for a few years after filming

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:59.310
wrapped on Ferris Bueller's Day Off and talked
about ideas for a sequel.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:04.129
According to Hughes, in those proposed movies,
Ferris would, quote, "be in college or at

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:07.149
his first job, and the same kind of things
would happen again."

00:01:07.149 --> 00:01:11.329
The two ultimately decided that a sequel like
that would basically be a lame rehash, because

00:01:11.329 --> 00:01:14.579
the original movie was, quote, "about a singular
time in your life."

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.291
“Incredible.

00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:21.659
One of the worst performances of my career
and they never doubted it for a second.”

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:23.389
John Hughes moved on

00:01:23.389 --> 00:01:28.139
Hughes created a string of classic teen movies,
including The Breakfast Club, Pretty in Pink,

00:01:28.139 --> 00:01:32.810
and Sixteen Candles —and Ferris Bueller's
Day Off was just about the last one.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:38.329
After Bueller, Hughes only made one more teen
movie in the '80s, 1987's Some Kind of Wonderful.

00:01:38.329 --> 00:01:42.170
Then he shifted to family fare like Uncle
Buck and Home Alone, and more grown-up films

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:45.520
such as She's Having a Baby and Planes, Trains,
and Automobiles.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.529
“Where’s your other hand?”

00:01:47.529 --> 00:01:48.679
“Between two pillows.”

00:01:48.679 --> 00:01:51.399
“Those aren’t pillows.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:52.760
Ahhh!”

00:01:54.109 --> 00:01:55.329
Busy Broderick

00:01:55.329 --> 00:01:59.029
Matthew Broderick was one of the biggest stars
of the late '80s, and a big part of that was

00:01:59.029 --> 00:02:01.530
because of his role in Ferris Bueller's Day
Off.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:06.359
In the five years afterwards, Broderick starred
in an eclectic bunch of movies, from contemporary

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:10.530
dramas like Torch Song Trilogy and Project
X to comedies like Family Business and The

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:15.720
Freshman, to period pictures like Biloxi Blues
and the Oscar-winning Civil War epic Glory.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.530
With all that on his plate, Broderick just
didn't have time to make a sequel.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.260
Hughes didn't dig sequels

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.580
There aren't many sequels in the Hughes filmography.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.310
While his screenplay for the first Vacation
movie is based on a true story he wrote up

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:31.471
for National Lampoon, he skipped having anything
to do with European Vacation — though he

00:02:31.471 --> 00:02:34.670
did return to write Christmas Vacation.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:40.640
“Ahh...It’s good.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:41.580
It’s good.”

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.420
That's the only sequel Hughes ever worked
on.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.040
He reportedly toyed with a follow-up to The
Breakfast Club for years — which would've

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.240
presumably been called "The Lunch Bunch" — but
couldn't ever come up a legitimate reason

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:55.390
to get those five characters back into the
same room again.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:58.870
Similarly, he just couldn't conceive a worthy
Ferris Bueller sequel.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:00.460
“You’re still here?

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:04.640
It’s over.

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:07.770
Go home.”

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.080
Second movie second thoughts

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:15.650
In spite of his sequel aversion, Hughes still
mapped out his ideas for Ferris Bueller 2.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:18.790
He shared some of them with Jeffrey Jones,
who played evil Principal Rooney.

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:23.650
At a Hughes tribute event after the director's
death in 2009, Jones claimed Hughes had told

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:27.400
him some of his ideas for Bueller 2, but all
Jones would say was that it was supposed to

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:31.260
be set in Hawaii, and that it fell apart because
Broderick ultimately wasn't interested in

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:33.260
reprising his role.

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.040
What about a sequel series?

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.700
In the fall of 1990, NBC debuted a single-camera
sitcom called Ferris Bueller, which operated

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:43.980
under the odd conceit that the show depicted
the real life characters on which the movie

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:48.190
version of Ferris Bueller's Day Off was based
on - and they hated the movie.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.060
“This is television.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:52.580
This is real.”

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:57.100
The show, which was pretty much a standard
high school sitcom notable only for the participation

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:01.650
of a young Jennifer Aniston as Bueller's sister
Jeannie, didn't attract much of an audience

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:05.290
and was cancelled halfway through the 1990-91
TV season.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:08.740
“Not to worry, Cameron.

00:04:08.740 --> 00:04:10.840
I fixed it.”

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:12.900
There were two sequels... sort of

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:17.049
Though there was no full length sequel, Matthew
Broderick did sort of reprise his role as

00:04:17.049 --> 00:04:18.870
Ferris Bueller on two occasions.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:24.310
In John Hughes' 1988 relationship comedy She's
Having a Baby, the end credits roll as a rapid-fire

00:04:24.310 --> 00:04:28.300
montage of celebrities throw out their ideas
for what Kevin Bacon and Elizabeth McGovern's

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:30.020
characters should name their new baby.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:34.490
Among the cameos is Matthew Broderick, clearly
in character as Ferris Bueller.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:35.490
“Jim.”

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:40.080
And in 2012, Broderick returned again for
a two-and-a-half minute Honda Super Bowl commercial

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.289
which features numerous nods to the movie.

00:04:42.289 --> 00:04:45.539
He may not have technically been playing Ferris
Bueller, but this could be the closest we

00:04:45.539 --> 00:04:46.810
ever get to a sequel.

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:47.960
“He bought it.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:52.440
How can I handle work on a day like today.”

00:04:52.449 --> 00:04:53.449
Fan script

00:04:53.449 --> 00:04:58.210
In 2011, an amateur screenwriter named Rick
Rapier went viral when he released the first

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.960
12 pages of a Ferris Bueller sequel script
he'd written.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:05.639
The plot: Ferris Bueller is now 40 years old
and is a motivational speaker, a la Tony Robbins.

00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:09.889
But he's incredibly overworked, so with his
business manager — Cameron Frye — he takes

00:05:09.889 --> 00:05:10.889
a day off.

00:05:10.889 --> 00:05:14.789
It couldn't gain much traction in Hollywood,
however — Rapier hadn't been hired by anyone

00:05:14.789 --> 00:05:16.669
to write the script, or even asked.

00:05:16.669 --> 00:05:20.129
The script was never purchased, and Rapier's
15 minutes of internet fame were up.

00:05:20.129 --> 00:05:22.240
“I changed my mind.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:23.620
I want you out in front of the school with
her.

00:05:23.620 --> 00:05:25.929
I’d like to have a few words with you, by
God.”

00:05:27.139 --> 00:05:29.159
Maybe they'll do one someday

00:05:29.159 --> 00:05:33.120
Alan Ruck, who played Ferris's best friend
Cameron, has spoken about a loose idea for

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.849
revisiting the movie's characters in the distant
future.

00:05:35.849 --> 00:05:40.449
The plot: Cameron, still a sad sack at 70,
commits himself to a nursing home even though

00:05:40.449 --> 00:05:42.310
he's in relatively good health.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:46.050
His old friend Ferris gets wind of it, busts
him out, and the two old men have a blast—going

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:48.710
into the city, hitting a strip club, et cetera.

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:50.419
At the end, Cameron dies.

00:05:50.419 --> 00:05:54.490
With Ferris Bueller 30 years in the past and
both Ruck and Broderick in their 50s now,

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:58.360
that means this Ferris Bueller Returns could
hit theaters sometime around 2036.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:02.120
“I said it before and I’ll say it again.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:03.439
Life moves pretty fast.

00:06:03.439 --> 00:06:07.579
You don’t stop and look around once in awhile,
you could miss it.”

00:06:08.289 --> 00:06:09.330
Thanks for watching!

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:11.789
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:11.789 --> 00:06:14.100
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

